Z||000011||JetroGT||08-26-2001||11:17 AM||||I think it looks good. I put black in my 2m4. I wanted to stencle the fiero pegasus on the grills too, but I was too lazy to make a stencle. ||||reg||14
Z||000012||Ryan Wright||08-27-2001||12:04 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>I wanted to stencle the fiero pegasus on the grills too, but I was too lazy to make a stencle. <HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Heh heh! I also thought about this but didn't want to go through the work, and I wasn't sure if I would be able to really make it look nice or not. So I just left it off.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>butI can't take out the back grille .What is the best way to do it??<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I figured they just popped off. Boy was I wrong. I slipped a flathead screwdriver up underneath each corner of the speaker cover, found the point where it attached, and gently pried it off. This broke the melted ends off and the covers came out. To put them back in, I used a knife to slightly trim down the ends of the four anchor points (so they'd go back in the holes in the interior trim panels) then pressed them in. They're thick enough up close to the grille that the pressure holds them in very well. I actually had to tap the corners to get 'em in.<P>Anyway, this method worked for me, but I only used it because I didn't know any better. I lucked out in that nothing broke (well, other than those melted ends, which had to go anyway) and I was able to get them back in with no problems. They're tight; I'm not sure I could get them back out without breaking them, to be honest. If I had to do it over again, I'd remove the interior trim pieces, then use a knife to cut the excess melted plastic off. Putting them back in, Monkeyman mentioned silicon, which would work great. Or a little glue. Or, you could use an old soldering iron to re-melt the ends, if there was enough plastic left.<P>I'm really surprised such a poor method was used to attach these. You'd think they could have come up with something a little more elegant.<P>Gargoyle: I'm not familiar Oreif or his (her?) door emblems. Where can I get more info?<P>Aus: Thanks for the link. It's a nice steering wheel, but I'm really partial to my old GT wheel. I swapped it into my new Fiero a couple of days ago and will probably use it until it flat out falls apart, in memory of my fallen '85...<P>------------------<BR>-Ryan Wright (http://www.ryanwright.com)||||reg||1
Z||000013||Monkeyman||08-27-2001||06:14 AM||||Ryan--Oreif is a him and he paints the little rectangular screw cover on your inner door handle assy.  Do a search for...hmmm.  Try to search for "Oreif".  Something like 5 bucks and you send him your old ones.  High quality, well worth the price.  I don't know why everyone doesn't have them.  I've bought two sets so far and I'll buy a set for any future Fiero.<P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM||||reg||1
